# Credentials for the Vercel Flags smoke tests. Copy to smoke/.env (gitignored).
#
# Two ways to authenticate. You need ONE of them.

# --- Option A: an SDK key (durable, works anywhere, no expiry) ---
# The Vercel Flags client reads this. It accepts either a bare key matching
# /^vf_(server|client)_/ or a connection string of the form
# "flags:sdkKey=vf_server_...".
#
# Get it from the Vercel dashboard under Flags > SDK Keys. Vercel also creates
# one per flag environment automatically when you make your first flag, and
# stores it in a FLAGS variable on the project, so `vercel env pull` may bring
# it down for you.
#
# NOTE: this is NOT the same as FLAGS_SECRET. FLAGS_SECRET is the Flags SDK's
# signing secret for the Flags Explorer and precompute. It is not a credential
# for flag evaluation and will not work here.
FLAGS=

# --- Option B: an OIDC token (short-lived, ~12 hours) ---
# Written by `vercel env pull`. Used only when FLAGS is empty: the client falls
# back to OIDC when it has no SDK key.
#
# Caveat: the token is refreshed by reading the Vercel CLI's stored credentials
# from disk and calling api.vercel.com. That refresh cannot happen inside the
# Supabase edge sandbox, so once this expires the edge run fails until you pull
# a fresh one. Prefer Option A for anything repeatable.
VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN=

# The flag key to resolve. Must exist in the Vercel project.
# Set it to ON in Vercel — the declared default here is `false`, so an ON flag
# is the only way to tell a real resolution from a silent fallback.
SMOKE_FLAG_KEY=

# Not used by the default (non-Next.js) entry point. Kept only to document that:
# @vercel/flags-core's own source says the default client "relies on
# process.env.FLAGS" and "does not use process.env.EDGE_CONFIG".
EDGE_CONFIG=
